course: ukrainian-street
lesson: 4
title: I don't understand — and what's your name?
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The most important sentence a guest can learn is ya ne rozuMIyu — I don't understand — and right after it shche raz, BUD laska, once more, please.
explain: Grown-ups are delighted when you try, and they will simply say it again slower or switch to English.
explain: In the playground a new kid asks yak teBE zvut? — literally «how do they call you?» — and you answer MEne zvut and your name.
explain: Ukrainian names change shape like clothes: one boy can be Oleksandr at school, Sashko with friends and Sashunya to his granny, and he is the same boy every time.
fact: zvychAino means of course, a slightly formal yes to a request from an adult; again the «chai» in it is not tea.
fact: ne znAyu means I don't know, often said with a shrug; ne means not.
fact: ya ne rozuMIyu means I don't understand; when a guest says it, people usually repeat more slowly or try English.
fact: shche raz, BUD laska means once more, please; raz is one time and shche is again or more.
fact: shcho? means what?; it is fine with friends, but to adults the politer pardon? is pereproshUyu?, and a bare shcho? to a granny can sound blunt.
fact: yak teBE zvut? means what's your name?, to a kid; literally it is «how do they call you?».
fact: MEne zvut ... means my name is ...; a guest gives his own English name, and the friends may make a Ukrainian version of it.
fact: yak vas zvut? is the polite what's your name?, but children rarely ask grown-ups this; they call adults by first name and father's name, or TItko, auntie.
fact: DUzhe pryYEmno means nice to meet you, said after introductions; the answer is meni tezh, me too.
fact: Oleksandr is a common boy's name; Sasha, Sashko and Sashunya are its short and pet forms.
fact: Dmytryk and DIma are pet forms of the boy's name Dmytro.
fact: Solomiyka and OlEnka are pet forms of the girls' names Solomiya and Olena; endings like -ka make a name affectionate.
fact: Calling a friend by his full name, like Oleksandr, can sound as if his mum is cross with him.
exercise: What does zvychAino mean? | Of course | I don't know | Usually not | Tea, please
exercise: Sashko asks where the remote is and you have no idea. What do you say? | ne znAyu | zvychAino | DUzhe pryYEmno | MEne zvut
exercise: What does ya ne rozuMIyu mean? | I don't understand | I don't remember it now | I'm not hungry, thanks | I don't want to
exercise: What do adults usually do when a guest says ya ne rozuMIyu? | Say it again slower or try English | Get cross and walk away | Laugh and stop talking | Shout the same words louder
exercise: What does shche raz, BUD laska mean? | Once more, please | One more sweet, please | Not now, please | Go away, please
exercise: In shche raz, what does raz mean? | One time | A razor for shaving | Quickly, hurry up | Right now
exercise: Why is a bare shcho? risky with a granny? | It can sound blunt; pereproshUyu? is nicer | It is a swear word that grannies really can't stand | It means no | It means goodbye
exercise: What does yak teBE zvut? mean? | What's your name? | How old are you now? | Where are you from? | How are you today?
exercise: What is the word-for-word meaning of yak teBE zvut? | How do they call you? | Who are you and where do you live? | Where is your name? | What do you want?
exercise: How do you answer yak teBE zvut? | MEne zvut and your name | DUzhe pryYEmno and nothing else | ne znAyu | zvychAino
exercise: Why do children rarely ask grown-ups yak vas zvut? | They call adults by name and father's name, or TItko | It is against the law | Adults have no names | It means «how old are you»
exercise: What does DUzhe pryYEmno mean? | Nice to meet you | Very tasty, thank you | Very tired after school | It is very far away
exercise: How do you answer DUzhe pryYEmno? | meni tezh | ne znAyu, poKA | shcho? shcho? | nE-a, DYAkuyu
exercise: Oleksandr, Sasha and Sashko are… | One name: full, short, pet | Three different boys from one class | A boy and his two older brothers | Names for a dog, a cat and a parrot
exercise: Dmytryk and DIma are pet forms of which name? | Dmytro | Daniel | Mykola | Ivan
exercise: Solomiyka and OlEnka are pet forms of… | Solomiya and Olena | Sofia and Olha | Oleksandr and Olena | Solomon and Oleh
exercise: What does the ending -ka in OlEnka add to the name? | Affection, a soft loving touch | Anger, a telling-off from Mum | A meaning of «old», like granny | A meaning of «boy»
test: «Sashko and Oleksandr are two different kids, obviously» Which is right? | No, Sashko is the pet form of Oleksandr — same boy | Yes, they are twins | Yes, Sashko is a girl's name | No, Sashko is the surname
test: Granny talks fast and you catch nothing. What helps most? | ya ne rozuMIyu, shche raz, BUD laska | shcho? shcho? shcho? | ne znAyu, poKA | Just nod and say AHA
test: A kid on the climbing frame says «yak teBE zvut?». You're Tom. What do you say? | MEne zvut Tom | DUzhe pryYEmno, Tom | Tom, zvychAino | yak vas zvut?
test: «OLEKSANDR! come here!» calls Sashko's mum. What does using the full name suggest? | She may be cross with him | She has forgotten his pet name | She is calling a different boy | She is being extra loving
test: Dmytryk says «DUzhe pryYEmno» after you meet. What do you reply? | meni tezh | neMA za shcho | ya ne rozuMIyu | do zAvtra
test: «grown-ups love it if u say yak vas zvut» What is right? | Kids rarely ask adults that; they use name and father's name or TItko | Yes, every kid asks adults their name | It is rude to say any name to adults | Adults must ask the kid first in writing
test: «oi, shcho?!» you snap at Sashko's grandad. What would be politer? | pereproshUyu? | shche shcho? | ne znAyu, LAdno | nE-a
test: Sashko asks «ty hraty budesh?» (will you play?) and you want to very much. Which answer fits? | zvychAino! | ne znAyu, poKA | ya ne rozuMIyu | DUzhe pryYEmno
test: «just shrug, ne znAyu is rude» Which is right? | No, ne znAyu is a normal «I don't know» | Yes, it is a rude word for adults only | Yes, it means «leave me alone, go away» | No, it means «of course»
test: In a cartoon a girl named Olena is called OlEnka by her granny. Why? | It is a loving pet form of Olena | Granny forgot her name and guessed | OlEnka is her sister | It means «naughty Olena»
test: «what's this kid's name — Solomiya? Solomiyka? make up your mind» What is the answer? | Both: Solomiyka is the loving form of Solomiya | Only Solomiyka is a real name, Solomiya is not | They are two girls | Solomiya is a boy's name
exam: Identify the utterance by which a visitor may indicate that he has not comprehended what was said. | ya ne rozuMIyu | ne znAyu | zvychAino | DUzhe pryYEmno
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the forms Oleksandr, Sasha and Sashko. | They are the full, short and affectionate forms of a single personal name | They designate three distinct individuals | They are three surnames of one family | They are the masculine, feminine and plural of one noun
exam: Ascertain the literal signification of the enquiry yak teBE zvut? | How do they call you? | Whence do you come? | What is your age? | What is your occupation?
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the manner in which children customarily address adults? | By the first name together with the father's name, or by a word such as TItko | By the adult's first name alone | By the enquiry yak vas zvut? on every occasion | By the surname without any title
